I very much agree.  I'll never, ever forget the amazing thrill I felt
when I got my first "What do you wish for? " prompt.  It was absolutely
perfect!  It was extremely exciting and intimidating, and I think it
was meant to be.  I must have stewed agitatedly by my computer for ten
minutes, trying to decide what would be a good response.  A menu would
certainly have taken away most, though certainly not all, of the thrill.

I find it odd that a player must be held accountable for an accidental
keypress, a real-world mistake having nothing whatever to do with
the character's competence in the world of nethack, yet some are
suggesting that he should not be held responsible for his (read "his
character's") ignorance of his surroundings.  You have to learn what to
eat, how to fight, how magic works, and so on.  Why should a thorough
knowledge of dungeon contents be presumed (or given away)?

Next thing you know, someone will suggest that the game should print messages
like "You see here a statue. Try chipping away at it with your pickaxe."


